/-!
# The MTT parabolic dimension bound in weight two at every positive level

Theorem statement: `MTT.Cohomology.parabolicH1_finrank_le_weight_two`
(`044654dc-ffee-42a9-bf7c-bf43fc2792ee`), by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07.

Proof: submission `56b1a713-ef28-4a10-ba51-3a8e82e95108`, by cbirkbeck, 2026-09-07 (ACCEPTED);
locally adapted.

For every positive integer N, the complex dimension of the first parabolic cohomology of Gamma_1(N)
with degree-zero homogeneous-polynomial coefficients is at most twice the dimension of its
weight-two cusp forms. This is the full weight-two case of the MTT mission's general dimension
frontier. Degree-zero polynomials are constant, so the proof compares the MTT cocycles with
scalar-valued parabolic characters. It includes all small levels and uses no period-map injectivity
assumption.

## Explanation of the source proof

# Weight two at every positive level

This proves the complete k=2 case of the mission's dimension frontier. It is
independent of the period-cocycle injectivity branch.

1. A degree-zero homogeneous polynomial is its constant coefficient. Hence
   the coefficient representation is trivial, and taking the constant
   coefficient turns a one-cocycle into an additive character of Gamma_1(N).
2. An integral determinant-one matrix whose trace has square four fixes a
   rational cusp. If its lower-left entry is zero, use infinity; otherwise
   use (a-d)/(2c). The determinant and trace identities verify the fixed-point
   polynomial directly. Scalar matrices are included, with no exceptional case
   omitted.
3. The MTT parabolic condition therefore makes the resulting character vanish
   on every trace-squared-four element. Constant-coefficient evaluation is
   injective, giving an embedding into the existing scalar parabolic-hom space.
4. The already-proved integral base-change theorem supplies bases of equal finite
   size for its integral and complex forms. The already-proved dimension bound
   for integral parabolic characters of a congruence subgroup then applies.
   Taking the quotient by principal cocycles cannot increase dimension.

